#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""PreToolUse guard for a hub workspace (hub.json + sibling repos).

deny  = hard block (secrets (guard.secret_env_files added, guard.readable_env_files released), force-push / push to the default branch, curl|sh, docker volumes, infra commands
        (with guard.infra: a production marker anywhere, or a part or one of its calls (also split at a single
        &, $( ), backticks, <( ), ( ), { }) with no allowed environment outside a ` #` comment and not a
        local-only form; without it: the fixed infra rule; both see a redirection glued to the tool word,
        aws>/dev/null; values in their reasons are escaped and capped), AI attribution in commits/PRs/branches,
        editing .git internals, network to guard.deny_hosts, reading or changing paths in guard.deny_paths,
        from a file tool or any Bash token; first of all, a blocked word (guard.blocked_words of hub.json,
        hub.local.json and $HUB_CONFIG together, ignoring ASCII case, no letter beside it) in the Bash
        command, raw, joined or with its quotes removed, in a scanned session variable's value (the infra
        selectors, the usual stage and connection names, credentials.env and guard.blocked_words_env names),
        in a file tool's path (file_path, notebook_path, path, Grep's glob, Glob's pattern, WebFetch's url),
        or in the contents of a .env* or guard.blocked_words_files file (as named or linked to) that a Read, a
        Grep (its path or the cwd walked, no links, BLOCKED_WALK_SKIPPED and .claude/worktrees skipped) or a
        Bash token (braces and globs expanded; not after ls, test, [, stat) reads; a secret file, by any case
        or link, is never opened, so it keeps the secret-file reason), or in any key or string value of the
        input of an input tool (WebSearch, Task, Agent, SendMessage, mcp__*; walked in order, the tool name
        not scanned; the only rule these tools get)
      right after it, a declared env file (credentials.env_file of an environment or an endpoint, in
        hub.json, hub.local.json or $HUB_CONFIG; in every session, HUB_RUN_ENVIRONMENT or not): a file tool
        on it (its path from the event's cwd, by real path, so a link or `..` is caught), a Grep on it or
        on the folder holding it, or whose non-empty glob can reach it, a Glob whose root holds one and
        whose pattern can reach it, or a Bash command or WebFetch URL
        naming it (its file name as a whole word, in the command's views; a Bash token, braces and globs
        expanded, or a file:// URL whose real path is it); the reason names the environment only
ask   = needs the user's confirmation (infra deploy/destroy/delete verbs on an allowed environment, anywhere
        after the tool (aws's s3 … rm and cloudformation … deploy too, quoted or not); with guard.infra, a
        command it would let through that holds a character other than ASCII letters, digits, space, tab,
        _ - . / : = @ , + % and the joins ; && || | newline, or a word that always asks: a shell, python*,
        perl, node, ruby, php, xargs, eval, exec, source, `.` as a command, env -i/-u, sudo, su, doas, --,
        parallel, a sed that runs text (e command or flag, -f), or a part that sets the environment twice,
        under one spelling or two),
        removing test assertions (`assert`, `expect(`, `pytest.raises`,
        `.assert_*(`, unittest's `.assertX(` and `self.fail(`) or adding skip/xfail/quarantine markers,
        deleting or sed-editing tests from Bash, editing the hub's guard files: plugin/hub-workflow/hooks/,
        plugin/<project>/hooks/, .claude/settings*.json, hub.lock, hub.json and hub.local.json, in the hub or a hub worktree,
        and the hub/agent launchers (a Bash write asks only when one is its target, so running them never asks);
        editing paths in guard.ask_before_edit, writing brain/ outside now.md, journal/, _inbox/, auto/, features/,
        a mutating hub env (add, set, remove, service add|remove; list and show pass),
        with HUB_RUN_ENVIRONMENT naming a declared environment of access read or production (an agent
        node of a workflow that declares it), a Bash command naming one of its endpoints' hosts or
        resources, or a WebFetch of a URL on one of its hosts (environment_endpoint, after the env-file
        rule; best effort: a command that hides a host passes; the reason names the environment only),
        and any Bash or file-tool call while blocked words cannot be checked: a command or a listed file over
        1 MiB, over 64 listed files or 4 MiB of them in one call, a listed file that is not regular, the
        rule's 300 ms spent, a blocked list of any layer unusable, or a hub.local.json that exists but is
        unusable; an unreadable listed file gets no verdict; an input tool's input over 1 MiB or 50000
        values), and a hook event that is not a JSON object
        while blocked words (or a blocked-list problem) are in effect; a call the env-file rule cannot
        check (the command over 1 MiB, too many words, its 300 ms spent; an unreadable event while an
        env file is declared)
Project values (default branch, tracker team, guard lists) come from hub.json. The branch prefix of a deny's
hint is the developer's: hub.local.json, else hub.json, else the local part of git config user.email plus `/`
(git runs only on that deny); with none, the hint has no prefix. A list entry `@hub/<path>` is <path> in the hub that holds this file; with no such hub (plugin cache) it is skipped.
Then, for Bash and the file tools (EXTENSION_TOOLS) and unless the verdict is deny (final), the project's extension plugin/<project>/hooks/project_guard.py
of the hub that holds this file (never one found from $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, the cwd or the tool input;
none in the plugin cache) runs in a child, project_guard_runner.py, with a 3 s timeout that kills its
whole process group. When that hub.json names no project (unreadable, or no project.name), the one
plugin/*/hooks/project_guard.py there is, if exactly one, runs. It only tightens: its ask or deny
replaces a weaker verdict, and anything else it does (another answer, extra output, a crash, the
timeout, a file that resolves outside the hub) is an ask naming the cause, added to a base ask's reason.
That guarantee covers a buggy extension, not a hostile one: it runs as the user, with the user's
rights, so its trust boundary is the ask on every edit of plugin/<project>/hooks/ (and of this folder).
Everything else passes through to the normal permission rules.
After a deny or an ask is printed, the guard sends it to the local control plane through the event
sensor's send_decision (event_sensor.py beside it): the hook input, its content reduced to sizes
(reduce_hook: no file content or edit strings leave the machine), with
decision {by: guard, kind, rule}, rule a stable id read from the reason (RULE_IDS). The send is
bounded by the sensor's 0.5 s, prints nothing and never changes the decision or the exit code.
"""

